CVE-2025-30153
CVE-2026-35469

# ── Round 3 (2026-07-24, GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf grpc-go < 1.82.1) ───────────
# Ground truth: a real trivy scan of the embedded image (CI run
# 30114848086) found the SAME advisory (GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf, published
# 2026-07-15, no CVE assigned yet, CVSS v4 8.8 HIGH -- an xDS RBAC
# authorization bypass via unsupported `Metadata`/`RequestedServerName`
# fields being silently ignored, an HTTP/2 Rapid Reset DoS mitigation
# bypass, and an xDS RBAC engine panic on malformed `NOT` rules; fixed
# upstream at grpc-go v1.82.1) across all 9 of the same opentofu/packer/
# terraform-provider binaries Round 1 (39c76c3) and Round 2 already
# bumped/verified. The fix line simply moved past every one of their
# vendored grpc-go versions: all six nixpkgs-security-escape-hatch
# binaries sat at v1.79.3 (one rung above the Round-1 CVE-2026-33186 fix
# line, one rung below this new one); porkbun/rabbitmq were already below
# even v1.79.3 (v1.67.1/v1.72.1).
#
# terraform-provider-aws and packer had real, fresh upstream fixes
# (v6.56.0 published 2026-07-22, v1.16.0 published 2026-07-24 -- both
# confirmed via `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/releases/latest` +
# `contents/go.mod?ref=<tag>`: both pin grpc v1.82.1) and are now sourced
# via the substrate cve-mitigations catalog
# (terraform-provider-aws-grpc-bump.nix, packer-grpc-bump.nix) rather than
# waiting on nixpkgs-security's channel to notice a release that was
# hours old -- closed for real, verified via `go version -m` on the built
# binary (grpc v1.82.1), NOT listed below.
#
# The remaining 7 -- terraform-provider-github (6.13.0), -cloudflare
# (5.22.0), -kubernetes (3.2.1), -random (3.9.0), -porkbun (0.3.0),
# -rabbitmq (1.10.1), and opentofu (1.12.4; the newer tag v1.12.5,
# published 2026-07-21, was checked too) -- each re-verified 2026-07-24
# via `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/releases` (the FULL list, not just
# `/latest`, to rule out an overlooked point release) AND a direct
# `contents/go.mod?ref=<tag>` fetch at each repo's newest tag: NONE has
# picked up grpc-go >= 1.82.1 yet (opentofu's v1.12.5 still pins v1.79.3
# -- the point release didn't touch grpc). Same "no upstream release
# exists to bump to; hand-patching go.mod/go.sum + a vendorHash recompute
# is materially riskier than a version bump and out of scope" reasoning as
# the prior two rounds (see the terraform-provider-random/-porkbun/
# -rabbitmq entries above). Re-check whenever any of these seven cuts a
# new release.
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf

# ── Round 4 (2026-07-24, GHSA-r277-6w6q-xmqw kin-openapi fail-open auth
# bypass) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The FIRST completed Trivy scan of this image (this build finally cleared
# the Round 3 hash-mismatch + stale-substrate-pin blockers) surfaced a new,
# unrelated CRITICAL finding: github.com/getkin/kin-openapi v0.111.0 (fixed
# v0.144.0) inside terraform-provider-kubernetes 3.2.1's own vendored
# dependency tree -- ValidationHandler.Load()'s NoopAuthenticationFunc
# default silently no-ops OpenAPI security-scheme validation. terraform-
# provider-kubernetes 3.2.1 is already confirmed (Round 3, above) as the
# latest upstream release with no newer tag to bump to; kin-openapi is
# still pre-1.0 (no semver guarantee) and the 0.111.0 -> 0.144.0 jump spans
# 33 minor releases -- a bare `go.mod replace` is the same "materially
# riskier than a version bump, out of scope" shape already accepted for
# this exact provider's grpc-go finding in Round 3, not a lower-risk case
# just because the vulnerable package is smaller. Re-check when
# terraform-provider-kubernetes cuts a release that bumps its own
# kin-openapi pin.
GHSA-r277-6w6q-xmqw

# Round 5 (2026-07-25, GHSA-jpcw-4wr7-c3vq kin-openapi nil-pointer panic in
# openapi3filter) -- same package, same 0.111.0 -> 0.144.0 gap as Round 4's
# GHSA-r277-6w6q-xmqw above -- a MEDIUM finding this time (an
# unauthenticated nil-pointer panic when openapi3filter validates a request
# against a `content` parameter whose media type has no schema).
#
# Unlike Round 4, this one was actually attempted, not just risk-assessed: a
# vendored go.mod patch (substrate's terraform-provider-kubernetes-kin-
# openapi-bump cve-mitigation, left in the catalog unwired as a record)
# bumped kin-openapi to v0.144.0 inside terraform-provider-kubernetes
# 3.2.1's own vendor tree. `go mod tidy` resolved the graph fine, but the
# provider's OWN source fails to compile against it (CI run 30178928379):
# `Schema.Type` changed from a plain string to `*openapi3.Types`, and the
# openapi2/openapi3 SchemaRef split changed shape -- manifest/openapi/
# foundry_v2.go and schema.go (both pre-existing, unrelated to this scan)
# still assume the old API. This CONFIRMS Round 4's risk assessment
# empirically rather than just asserting it.
#
# Separately, and more load-bearing than the compile failure alone: this
# specific CVE's vulnerable function (openapi3filter.ValidateRequest) is
# never called anywhere in terraform-provider-kubernetes's source tree
# (confirmed via a full recursive grep of the v3.2.1 tree, zero matches for
# "openapi3filter") -- the provider only uses kin-openapi for OpenAPI
# schema TYPE modeling (resolving CRD manifest schemas), never for HTTP
# request validation. The vulnerable code path is unreachable in this
# binary's actual usage, independent of whether the version gets bumped.
# Re-check when terraform-provider-kubernetes cuts a release that bumps its
# own kin-openapi pin past 0.144.0 while staying source-compatible with
# this provider's manifest/openapi package.
GHSA-jpcw-4wr7-c3vq
